{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed: after renaming back to Indent2.md, the favourite reappears. So the mark remained stored under the old path during the rename; the renamed file lost the mark, and after renaming back the mark is visible again. This is a clear stale-mark bug (\"quick-access marking not kept up to date on rename\"). Great, I'll report it.\n\nNow, before filing, let's decide element_ref and observed_quote. The defect is best shown on the Favourites screen being empty after rename, while Indent2x.md existed. But observed_quote could be the empty hint \"¯\\_(ツ)_/¯\" on Favourites screen. However, per instructions, report about missing may leave quote out; but quote must have been on screen. The empty hint was on screen. But the element_ref should point at the element showing the defect, from latest observe. Latest observe is Favourites showing Indent2.md (after rename back) — not the defect state. I can file the report referring to screen \"Favourites\" with element maybe the empty hint from the earlier observation, but element numbers must be from the *latest* observation. Hmm, \"element_ref: number of the element showing the defect, from your latest observe\". So I need to be on the defect screen when filing. I can reproduce the missing-state again? That would require favourite -> rename -> Favourites. We've already restored. Rather than redo, I can navigate back to re-create the defect state: currently Favourites shows Indent2.md from the stale mark. If I rename Indent2.md to Indent2x.md again (in main list), then Favourites empty and I can file while observing Favourites with empty hint element ref 7.\n\nBut that means more steps and another rename/restore. Alternatively file report now with screen \"Favourites\", element \"Favourites file list\", element_ref from latest observe (7? which is Indent2.md currently) — but that would show the mark present, not the defect. The developer may reject.\n\nLet me reproduce the defect one final time and keep it on screen while filing:\n1. Unfavourite Indent2.md now (to clean starting state), or keep favourite. Actually to reproduce defect: from Favourites, long-press Indent2.md → Rename isn't available in Favourites view (only Favourite, Details, Delete). Hmm rename isn't in Favourites. Need to go to Documents and rename there.\n\nCurrent state: Indent2 favourite still set (stale but consistent now). Plan: go to Documents, rename Indent2.md → Indent2x.md, then go Favourites (empty), observe, file report. Then afterwards rename back and unfavourite to restore. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, since we already have proof twice, filing while on the \"empty Favourites\" state is worthwhile. Let's do the third repro (it will be quick) and file.\n\nActually, wait. Maybe the app's rename failure is intended semantics: favourites are per-path; renaming effectively makes it a new file; Markor historically removes favourites on rename? Hmm. Let me consider the hint again: \"File browser quick-access marking: the browser's marks are now kept up to date by its own handlers alone.\" Maybe \"marks\" refers to something else entirely: quick access could be the \"Recent\" files list? \"marks\" could be the little file-type icons? Let me examine if there's any visible \"mark\" state in the browser besides favourites. Long-press menu shows Favourite. There's no other mark. \"Quick-access\" likely the Favourites folder in \"Go to\". So yes.\n\nHow does Markor handle rename of favourites in real code? Let me recall Markor's source (net.gsantner.markor). There is `AppSettings.getFavouriteFiles()` which returns a set. In `FileBrowserFragment`/`FilesystemListFragment`, when renaming, I believe they do:\n```\nif (AppSettings.get().getFavouriteFiles().contains(oldPath)) {\n    AppSettings.get().getFavouriteFiles().remove(oldPath);\n    AppSettings.get().getFavouriteFiles().add(newPath);\n}\n```\nNot sure, but plausible, because a good app would preserve. Also Markor has \"quick access\" list maybe called \"quick access files\" and \"marks\" might be a set of \"favourites\". The hint implies the file browser should keep them updated itself on rename/delete. So a missing update is a real regression.\n\nI'm fairly confident. Let's reproduce once more and file.\n\nActually, could there be a bug where the favourite disappears from Favourites even without rename, after leaving and returning? Earlier we saw Indent2.md appeared in Favourites via Go to. That worked. So mark itself works. Only rename breaks it.\n\nLet me do the final repro and file. Go back to Documents. From Favourites, press BACK → /storage → tap Notebook."}
