{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting! Replace all produced \"Search or replace pattern error.\" and content unchanged. That is a potential bug: In Java/Android regex, `$1` group references work in Matcher.replaceAll; but the app may convert replace string using `$` escaping; `$2@$1` should be valid. Error indicates the replacement pattern parsing failed. Hmm.\n\nWait, is regex replacement syntax in this search bar different? Maybe this search is based on the \"SearchBar\" library (Markor uses a custom SearchBar from `com.github.quillraven...`? Actually Markor's editor uses \"SearchBar\" from `com.github.axet:...`?). The result text \"Search or replace pattern error.\" is from Markor's search implementation. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the error arises because the replacement pattern is treated as a regex too and `$` is special? In Markor's `SearchReplace` code (from `com.github.gsantner.markor...` / `org.woheller69...`?), I recall Markor's search bar is derived from `com.github.gsantner.markor.frontend.textview.SearchBar`. It uses `Pattern.compile` on both search and replace? Let me think. In Markor's source, `SearchBar` is based on \"AmbilWarna\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, I recall Markor uses `SearchBar` from `com.github.gsantner.markor.frontend.textview` maybe adapted from `com.github.axet.androidlibrary`. There's a \"replaceAll\" that does `Pattern.compile(search).matcher(text).replaceAll(replace)`.\n\n`$2@$1` should be valid. But the result error might be because of \"Use Regular Expression\" toggled? Wait—did tapping the regex icon actually enable regex? The result was 2/2 for `[a-z]+@[a-z]+`, so yes regex is on. Replace with `$2@$1` should be valid Java replacement. Unless the app escapes `$` somewhere or treats replacement as regex via `Matcher.quoteReplacement`.\n\nHmm, maybe the replace string is passed through `Regex` replacement where \"$\" handling requires `\\` escapes? Let's test simpler regex replace: search `([a-z]+)@([a-z]+)` replace `$1` should produce \"cat dog\". If error also, then group refs unsupported/broken. But maybe this is intended limitation: Markor might not support group references, instead only literal replacement? Let me check via simple replace: search `cat` replace `dog` (regex on) should work. Let's test that; then test `$1`.\n\nBut before, maybe the error is due to the combination `@` in replacement? `$2@$1`: `@` literal, `$2` group 2 referenced. Should be fine.\n\nCould the \"Search or replace pattern error.\" be a normal error message when regex is invalid? Hmm.\n\nWait, did I possibly not enable regex? `[a-z]+@[a-z]+` as literal would find 0 results, but we got 2/2, so regex on.\n\nMaybe replacement uses `$` only with braces `${1}`? Let's test. We can test and revert. Let's try replace `$2@$1` with `${2}@${1}`? Hmm.\n\nBut note we already recorded \"Search or replace pattern error.\" — if that's a real bug (regex replace with capture groups fails), it's within the search/replace area and worth reporting. However, we need to verify it's not expected. Let me test Java-style `$1` in a simpler regex search to see error vs success.\n\nFirst clear search & replace, set search `([a-z]+)@([a-z]+)` and replace `$1`. Actually search pattern currently `[a-z]+@[a-z]+` has no groups, so `$1` would be invalid group reference → error expected! Ah, that's it. `$2@$1` references groups 2 and 1, but the pattern has no capturing groups, so it's a legit error. Right! My mistake. The error is correct behavior: \"Search or replace pattern error.\" because group references don't exist. Good, not a bug.\n\nLet's test with capturing groups to verify group replacement works. Change search to `([a-z]+)@([a-z]+)`, replace `$2@$1`. Clear search field and type new pattern; same for replace. Use type_text with clear true on element 14. But keyboard is open. Let's tap search field? type_text element 14 should tap."}
