{"type": "thinking", "text": "The table renders correctly, with escaped pipe, bold header/body, and missing cell padded. GFM table extension works.\n\nPotential table bug: header cells centered? Header \"A\" and \"B\" appear centered while default alignment in GFM is left. In the earlier table without alignment, \"Head A\", \"Head B\" appeared left-aligned (screenshot showed left). Here \"A\" and \"B\" appear centered — why? Hmm, because the column width is wide and \"A\" centered? Actually the header in a GFM table is left-aligned by default; the screenshot shows \"A\" centered in column. That's odd. Wait, in the earlier screenshot, \"Head A\" was left aligned. Maybe because single-letter content centered? No. Hmm, maybe the renderer centers header cells when...? Let me not overthink; minor.\n\nCould be a bug: header text is centered instead of left aligned, whereas body left aligned. But GFM default alignment is left for both header and body. The first table screenshot: \"Head A\" appeared left-aligned (padding ~10px). Let me compare: In second table, \"A\" is at x~136 in a column starting at x~60 width ~180, so centered. In first table, \"Head A\" at x~78 in a column starting at 60 width ~165, left. Hmm, maybe the renderer centers a cell when the content is short? Unlikely. Or maybe \"A\" and \"B\" are center-aligned because of the header separator row being \"---\" (left) ... no.\n\nWait, in the first table, we used `| Head A | Head B |` with separator `| --- | --- |`. In the second, `| A | B |`. Both default left. Hmm.\n\nActually maybe the accessibility bounds reported center of text but not alignment. Screenshot shows A centered in the first cell visually? The first cell spans x=60..240; \"A\" at x~135-160; so centered. Body \"a | b\" left at x~67. So header is centered, body left. Interesting: perhaps Markor's GFM table renderer makes header centered when the header row uses no leading/trailing pipes? No.\n\nLet's test a table with header `| Left | Left2 |` and body both long. In earlier first table, header \"Head A\" looked left-aligned. Let me compare precisely: first screenshot (sha 0a59...): first column from x≈60 to 223; \"Head A\" starts at x≈78. Left aligned. Second screenshot (81a9...): column from 60 to 242, \"A\" centered at ~135-160. Hmm why difference? The first table had `| Head A | Head B |` and second `| A | B |`. Both same syntax.\n\nCould the centering be due to GFM table extension parsing alignment from the separator row? Both separators were `---`. So no.\n\nHmm, maybe the renderer centers header cells only if the text is short? Or maybe in the second table, the header row is being recognized as alignment \"center\" due to the \"---\" being parsed... no.\n\nWait maybe the second table's header is `A` and `B`, but the first body row is \"a \\| b\" which is longer; table cell widths are computed. Header \"A\" might be centered by default in Markdown tables? But first table's header \"Head A\" left. Unless the first screenshot's \"Head A\" was actually centered too: column x=60-223, center=141; \"Head A\" width ~95, so starts at 93 if centered; actual looked maybe 78. Hard to tell. Let me measure the image mentally: The screenshot showed \"Head A\" starting near x=76 (a bit right of border at 60) → left-ish. In second, \"A\" starts around x=136 → centered. So yes different.\n\nMaybe there's a weird bug where single-letter cell content gets centered because the GFM table extension's alignment column parsing is off. But honestly this is cosmetic and uncertain.\n\nLet's test another table with short headers and longer body to see if header centering consistent. Not important.\n\nWhat about GFM table extension specific feature: tables without a leading pipe; we tested. Alignment colons: tested. Escaped pipes: tested. All good.\n\nI think view mode area is fine. Maybe test view mode on non-Markdown (todo.txt) — view mode might be disabled for txt? Let's see later.\n\nNow, one thing I want to check: the \"View Mode\" search function. In view mode, tapping magnifier opens a search? Let's test; could be part of view mode. Also \"Open with\" and \"Jump to bottom\". Not core.\n\nLet's test view mode on Shopping.md/Notes.md to ensure no crashes. Then exit.\n\nActually, more valuable: test the sort options Date and \"Folder local\"/\".dotfiles\" maybe. Since \"file browser and its sort options\" is area 1, I should exercise each sort option: Name, Date, Size, MIME type, Folder first, Reverse order, .dotfiles, and \"Folder local\". I tested Name, Size, Folder first, Reverse. Let me test Date, MIME, .dotfiles, and \"Folder local\".\n\n- Date: all files except our test files have same 1:02 date; our QAUndo/QAEmpty have 1:08/1:14. Sorting by Date should put QAUndo latest. But the sort direction? Let's test.\n- MIME: all .md files are same MIME (text/markdown?), todo.txt text/plain. Sorting by MIME should group.\n- \".dotfiles\": with folder containing no dotfiles, toggling should show nothing new. Could still test state persists.\n- \"Folder local\": unclear. Perhaps it's \"Folder first\" local vs global? Actually \"Folder local\" might be \"Show only local folder\"? Hmm. Let me inspect by tapping it and seeing effect. When I first opened Sort by, \"Folder local\" was unchecked. Clicking it may enable \"Folder local\" sort meaning folders sorted within? Hmm.\n\nLet me test these with screenshots/observes. Currently in view mode; exit to browser via BACK. Then sort dialog.\n\nBut wait, before that, we have test files QAUndo (now table) and QAEmpty. Sorting by Date will show them distinctly. Good.\n\nExit view mode: press BACK."}
